<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vanuatu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vanuatu is an island nation in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, formerly known as the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>. It was governed as an Anglo-French condominium. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> note that Vanuatu's independence in 1980 completed the process of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vanuatu.jpg" alt="Vanuatu" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kalontas Mahlon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vanuatu_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vanuatu is a Y-shaped archipelago of about 83 relatively small islands of volcanic origin, 65 of them inhabited, with about 1,300 kilometres between the most northern and southern islands. It lies 1,750 km east of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and 540 km northeast of New Caledonia. The largest island is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-santo">Espiritu Santo</a>, and the highest point is Mount Tabwemasana, at 1,879 metres. Most islands are steep, with unstable soils and little permanent freshwater. There are several active volcanoes, including Lopevi and Mount Yasur, and volcanic activity is common.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history before European colonisation is mostly obscure, since no written sources predate contact and little archaeological work has been done. Evidence gathered since the 1980s supports the settlement of the islands about 3,000 years ago, between roughly 1100 BCE and 700 BCE, by people almost certainly of the Lapita culture. Notable Lapita sites include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teouma">Teouma</a> on Éfaté, which has a large ancient cemetery containing the remains of 94 individuals. Sites on Éfaté, Lelepa and Eretoka are associated with the 16th–17th century chief or chiefs called Roy Mata, a title possibly held by different men over several generations, who according to oral tradition united local clans and presided over an era of peace.</p><p>The first Europeans to visit were a Spanish expedition led by the Portuguese navigator Fernandes de Queirós, who reached Espíritu Santo in 1606 and claimed the archipelago for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as La Austrialia del Espíritu Santo. James Cook named it the New Hebrides in 1774, after the Hebrides of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In the 1880s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom claimed parts of it, and in 1906 they agreed to manage it jointly as an Anglo-French condominium. An independence movement arose in the 1970s, and the Republic of Vanuatu was founded in 1980. The name combines vanua, meaning land or home, and tu, to stand; the first prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-lini">Walter Lini</a>, said it was meant to connote a country that has stood alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vanuatu is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> with a written constitution, and its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-vila">Port Vila</a> on Efate. The official languages are Bislama, English and French, and Bislama, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> with a mostly English-derived vocabulary, is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>. The Lapita sites became the country's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2008. A national Council of Chiefs, the Malvatu Mauri, advises the government on culture and language, and chiefs remain leading figures at village level. The country is a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a> and the Pacific Islands Forum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vanuatu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vanuatu</b>'s independence in 1980 completed the process.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Pohnpei, a high volcanic island of about 334 square kilometres in the eastern Caroline Islands, was probably settled around the start of the Common Era by people of the Lapita cultural tradition from the south-east Solomon Islands or <b>Vanuatu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudeleur-dynasty">Saudeleur dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the Bismarcks, Lapita voyagers passed by the main Solomon Islands, long inhabited, where few Lapita sites are known, and became the first people of the Reef and Santa Cruz Islands, <b>Vanuatu</b>, New Caledonia, Fiji, Tonga and Samoa.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The New Hebrides became independent as <b>Vanuatu</b> on 30 July 1980.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-hebrides-condominium">New Hebrides Condominium</a></li><li><blockquote>It had a small elected council and an elected president, and it relied on the goodwill of planters and traders in a population of a few hundred Europeans and many more ni-<b>Vanuatu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-franceville">Republic of Franceville</a></li></ul></section>
